Publish member sites, and make delisting take them down
Next step after the template split: `--publish` copies each built tenant into
`site/member-sites/<name>/` and rewrites `site/_data/member_sites.json`. The
piece `docs/deploying.md` described.

## The behaviour that matters is not the copying

**Removing an entry from `sites.yml` now takes the site down.** `--publish`
prunes published directories that are no longer listed. Without that, *"delisting
is the whole withdrawal"* is a thing we say rather than a thing we do, and a
published tree that only ever grows keeps serving somebody who asked us to stop.

That is the governance promise from `TENANCY.md` becoming a line of code, and it
is the reason this PR is worth reviewing carefully rather than skimming — it is
a recursive delete driven by a config file.

### Three guards on it

- **Only direct subdirectories** of the publish root are considered, so a
  listing page or a `.gitkeep` at the root survives.
- **The root must resolve inside the repository**, checked on the *shape of the
  path* before anything is read from disk.
- **`--only` prunes nothing at all.** A single-site run sees one entry, so every
  other site would look unlisted. That would take every member's site down at
  once and it is the worst bug available here, so it is spelled out in the code
  and has its own end-to-end test.

### A test caught a real ordering flaw

The first version checked *existence* before *containment*, so a path that both
escaped the repository and did not happen to exist returned quietly instead of
refusing — the guard passing precisely when it had least information. Fixed, and
the test that found it is in the suite.

## Two other decisions worth naming

**A failed tenant keeps what it published last time.** The cadence promises that
new work appears, not that old work vanishes the first morning somebody's data
file will not parse.

**A site publishes under its `name`, not its path.** The name is the public
address — subdomain label and folder. The path is where we happen to check their
repository out. Separating them means moving a checkout cannot silently change
somebody's address.

## The listing is data, and has no timestamp

`site/_data/member_sites.json`, written as data rather than as a page so FCPM's
own site can render it with a Liquid loop. **What that page says, and in whose
voice, is not this script's business** — same reason the template has no README.

No timestamp in it, because `sync-feeds.py` already learned that one: a payload
recording when it ran differs on every run and commits a file every week to say
it looked. The commit is the timestamp. Verified byte-stable across two runs.

## Two latent bugs fixed on the way

`runner=subprocess.run` and `path=MANIFEST` were **bound at function
definition**, so neither could be overridden by a caller. Invisible until a test
tried to drive `main()` over a synthetic repository and got the real `sites.yml`
instead. Both now resolve at call time.

## What is still not done, and why

**No workflow.** The cadence run is `python3 bin/build-sites.py --publish`,
ready to schedule. I have left the schedule alone twice now for the same reason
— you removed a deploy workflow specifically so two publish paths could not be
confused, and adding a scheduled committing one is a decision about how this
repository publishes, not a detail.

**No wildcard rewrite.** #90 laid out the two ways and they cost different
things: `run_worker_first` on the site Worker costs the main site its *"no main,
no runtime, nothing to execute"* property, and a second Worker costs a second
deploy. That is yours to pick.

**`site/member-sites/` is empty**, because there are no tenants yet. With none
listed, `--publish` is a no-op that writes an empty listing, which is the
correct behaviour and is what the committed `member_sites.json` shows.

## Verified

- `python3 bin/test_build_sites.py` — **44 tests, OK**. Includes `main()` end to
  end over a synthetic repo with two tenants: publishing lands both, delisting
  one takes it down, `--only` takes nothing down, and a failed tenant keeps its
  previous publish.
- `check-template.py` round trip passes; `test_no_secrets.py` 12 OK;
  `test_nothing_internal_is_published.py` 4 OK.
- Real run against this repository builds both sites and publishes zero.
- Tree clean after a full run apart from the intended listing file.

